      "dom_id": "question-66279",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\,{x}^{2}+5=-2x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+5=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=5.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-1+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-1-2\\,\\text{i}.$",
